# Processes as data — and the Arrow interface, said once.
#
# `examples/arrows.braid` shows that Control.Arrow's INTERFACE is just
# Braid's syntax: `arr f` is any pure program, `f >>> g` is `f ; g`,
# `first f` is `f _`, `f *** g` is `f g`, `app` is `ev`. That covers
# every arrow whose category is the one Braid already computes in.
#
# The effect-shaped arrows are not new categories either: the state
# arrow is a threaded `resource`, the Kleisli/Maybe arrow is the railway
# sum track, the writer arrow is a Log resource, nondeterminism is List.
# Only IO is irreducible, and it is a GRADE on the arrow (`=IO>`) rather
# than an arrow of its own (design-effects.md).
#
# What is genuinely left over is arrows that are PROCESSES: Yampa's
# signal functions, Hughes' stream transducers, circuits. Those really
# are a different category — and in Braid they are an ordinary
# recursive data type whose composition is an ordinary word.
#
# ---- what changed (2026-09-09) --------------------------------------
# This file used to end "nothing abstracts over the category; there is
# no `Arrow` class here, because Braid has no higher kinds to write one
# with." The second half was wrong, and the theory below is the
# correction. A theory parameter may now be a type CONSTRUCTOR —
# `theory Arrow(k(_, _))`, the arity written as underscores because a
# bare name already means a wire and `...` already means a stack. `k`
# is not a type and never reaches inference: it lives in the slot
# signatures, and the model head names a declared data type that is
# substituted for it before any slot is checked. That is the ML-functor
# move, not higher kinds — `Ty` still has no constructor variable, and
# nothing is inferred or dispatched. Slots also carry their OWN
# variables now (`a b c` below), each generalized per slot.

data Circuit(a, b) = Fn⟨a =Recursive> b Circuit(a, b)⟩
data Pair(a, b) = a b

# The step function is `=Recursive>`: every circuit below is written
# under `with Recursive`, and a written arrow means its manifest
# (MANUAL §14). The slots say so
# too — a slot declares the MOST a model may do, and the pure model
# below passes under a `=Recursive>` slot by absorption.
#
# What the slots DO NOT say, since 2026-09-12: nothing about the
# PROGRAM handed to `embed`. Composition joins grades now, so `arrC`
# (which ties a knot) no longer demands that its argument
# recurse; `Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩` is what the embedding takes, and `Arr` wraps the
# same pure arrow. Both spellings were `=Recursive>` while composition
# unified grades, and both were wrong.

# `Fn` itself cannot fill `k`: it is built in, it takes an ARROW rather
# than two wires, and a constructor parameter must name a declared data
# type. One line of wrapping buys it — and gives the theory a second,
# deliberately boring model to be audited against.
data Arr(a, b) = Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩

# ---- the theory ------------------------------------------------------
# `in Doctrine` is the whole of it (2026-09-13). `Doctrine` is the
# prelude's theory of arrows — `compose`, `embed`, `first`, and the
# seven arrow laws — and a theory that says `in Doctrine` DECLARES
# that its slots of those names are the doctrine's. The elaborator
# checks the claim against the written signatures and then knows,
# without reading a shape or guessing, that `with Circuits` transports.
#
# Before this the three slots were DETECTED by matching arrow shapes,
# and the names were this file's taste. Detection answered "does this
# theory happen to look like a category"; the declaration answers "does
# it claim to be one", which is the question `in` was invented for.
#
# `first` is still the slot that made constructor parameters necessary:
# its type mentions `k` at three different pairs of wires, and it names
# the pairing — `Pair` here, the doctrine's `p` — that packs a wide
# stage into one object.
#
# `observe` and `sample` are the audit's evidence, and they are the
# doctrine's slots too. A circuit is codata, so it cannot be compared,
# only OBSERVED; the doctrine asks for the observation, which is what
# keeps its laws honest about being finite tests of an infinite object.
# A theory that declares neither gets no laws — see `Vault` below.
theory Arrow(k(_, _)) in Doctrine =
    embed   : Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩ =Recursive> k(a, b)
    compose : k(a, b) k(b, c) =Recursive> k(a, c)
    first   : k(a, b) =Recursive> k(Pair(a, c), Pair(b, c))
    observe : k(Int, Int) =Recursive> Int
    sample  : • =Recursive> k(Int, Int)

# ---- model one: circuits ---------------------------------------------
# A circuit consumes an input and returns an output AND ITS SUCCESSOR.
# Recursion through Fn is what makes it codata (cf. examples/stream.braid).
def stepC = (c x -> c ; unCircuit ; _ x ; ev)

# the identity circuit, `arr`, `>>>`, and `first`
def idC with Recursive = [(x -> x (idC))] ; Circuit
def arrC with Recursive = (g -> [(x -> g x ; ev ; (y -> y (g ; arrC)))] ; Circuit)
def compC with Recursive = (f g -> [(x -> f x ; stepC ; (y f2 -> g y ; stepC ; (z g2 -> z (f2 g2 ; compC))))] ; Circuit)
def firstC with Recursive = (f -> [(p -> p ; unPair ; (x d -> f x ; stepC ; (y f2 -> (y d ; Pair) (f2 ; firstC))))] ; Circuit)

# A circuit that CANNOT be a pure function: its output depends on every
# input it has already seen. This is the whole reason Arrows exist.
def sumFrom with Recursive = (n -> [(x -> n x ; + ; dup ; _ (sumFrom))] ; Circuit)
def scale with Recursive = (n -> [(x -> (n x ; *) (n ; scale))] ; Circuit)

# feed 3 then 4, and report both outputs summed — two steps, because one
# step cannot tell a stateful circuit from a stateless one
def probe = (c -> c 3 ; stepC ; (y c2 -> c2 4 ; stepC ; (z c3 -> (y z ; +) (c3 ; drop))))

model Circuits in Arrow(Circuit) =
    embed   = arrC
    compose = compC
    first   = firstC
    observe = probe
    sample  = 0 ; sumFrom

# ---- model two: plain functions --------------------------------------
# The boring model, and the point of having one: the same seven laws
# run again over a carrier with no state at all.
def arrA     = Arr
def thenA    = (f g -> [(x -> f ; unArr ; _ x ; ev ; (y -> g ; unArr ; _ y ; ev))] ; Arr)
def firstA   = (f -> [(p -> p ; unPair ; (x d -> f ; unArr ; _ x ; ev ; (y -> y d ; Pair)))] ; Arr)
def runA     = (f x -> f ; unArr ; _ x ; ev)
def observeA = (f -> (f 3 ; runA) (f 4 ; runA) ; +)

model Funcs in Arrow(Arr) =
    embed   = arrA
    compose = thenA
    first   = firstA
    observe = observeA
    sample  = [dup ; +] ; Arr

# ---- generic code, written once --------------------------------------
# The body is the same twice; only the model differs.  `in` puts the
# model's slot words in scope and APPLIES NOTHING — resolution once per
# scope, never a dictionary per call.
def twiceC in Circuits = sample sample ; compose ; observe
def twiceA in Funcs = sample sample ; compose ; observe

twiceC ; print              # 13  — a running sum fed into a running sum:
                            #       3 ⇒ 3 ⇒ 3, then 4 ⇒ 7 ⇒ 10
twiceA ; print              # 28  — doubling twice: 3⋅4 + 4⋅4

# and the composite really is stateful: sum, then double
(0 ; sumFrom) (2 ; scale) ; compC
_ 3  ; stepC ; (y c -> (y ; print) c)      # 6
_ 4  ; stepC ; (y c -> (y ; print) c)      # 14
_ 10 ; stepC ; (y c -> (y ; print) c)      # 34
drop

# ---- composition, OWNED: `with` a model that has a carrier -------------
# `in Circuits` is a VOCABULARY: it puts this model's `embed`, `compose`
# and `first` in scope and leaves the plumbing to you.  `with Circuits`
# goes one step further and takes over `;`.
#
# The theory above DECLARES that: `in Doctrine`, plus the three slots
# at the doctrine's signatures — `compose` is `k(a,b) k(b,c) ⇒ k(a,c)`,
# `embed` is `Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩ ⇒ k(a,b)`, `first` is `k(a,b) ⇒ k(Pair(a,c),
# Pair(b,c))` — and that is the whole test.  The names are the
# doctrine's, which is why they are these names and not others: one
# spelling per thing.  `with Circuits` is then the functor out of the free
# category that sends every stage to the embedding of that stage and
# every `;` to the composition.  It is a LABEL WITH A CARRIER: it mints
# the receipt `Circuits` exactly as any functor scope does, and the
# carrier is the hom-object `Circuit(a, b)`.  The display folds the two
# together — a transported word is `• ⇒ Circuit(Int, Int)` carrying
# `Circuits` at the base, and prints `Int =Circuits Recursive> Int`.  Objects
# are shared with the base category; composition is the model's own.

def add1 = _ 1 ; +
def dbl  = 2 _ ; *

# The same pipeline twice.  `hand` is what `with` writes out for you;
# `easy` is what you write.
def hand in Circuits = [add1] ... ; embed ... ; _ [dbl] ; _ embed ; compose
def easy with Circuits = add1 ; dbl
#   easy : Int =Circuits Recursive> Int

# ENTERING is a marker, LEAVING is a model.  `observe` takes the carrier
# and returns base — an EXIT, read off its declared arrow — so it is
# refused inside `with Circuits` ("`observe` leaves Circuits") and called
# under `in Circuits`, where nothing is transported.  That rule is
# what makes the K-word table exact: every word written under `with` or
# `in` produces a carrier unless it deliberately exits, so the
# elaborator never has to ask inference which is which.
def handOut in Circuits = hand ; observe
def easyOut in Circuits = easy ; observe

handOut ; print             # 18 — (3+1)·2 + (4+1)·2
easyOut ; print             # 18 — the same pipeline, transported

# A transported word is an ordinary word, so it composes in the category
# with any other: inside `with Circuits`, `easy` is left alone and the
# `;` around it is `compose`.
def twice2 with Circuits = easy ; easy
def twice2Out in Circuits = twice2 ; observe
twice2Out ; print           # 40 — 3 ⇒ 8 ⇒ 18, 4 ⇒ 10 ⇒ 22

# OUTSIDE the scope, two of them side by side are two circuits side by
# side: `• =Circuits Recursive> Circuit(Int, Int) Circuit(Int, Int)`.  That is
# well-typed and honestly not composition in Circuits — the display fold
# wants exactly ONE carrier, so it does not fire and you can see the
# two.  Composition is written under the marker.
def pair    = easy easy
def pairOut in Circuits = pair ; compose ; observe
pairOut ; print             # 40 — the same composite, spelled by hand

# ---- wider stages: the STRENGTH is the padding ------------------------
# A hom-object `k(a, b)` names ONE wire on each side, so a transported
# spine is one carrier wire.  A stage that is not one-in-one-out is
# packed with the pairing the strength names — the elaborator reads
# `Pair` off `first`'s declared type, nothing is built in — and a stage
# narrower than the running width is whiskered by `first` once per wire
# riding above it.  That is resource routing's `_`-padding with `first`
# in place of `_`.
#
# So `dup ; *` transports, and squares:
def sq with Circuits = dup ; *
#   sq : Int =Circuits Recursive> Int
def sqOut in Circuits = sq ; observe
sqOut ; print               # 25 — 3² + 4²

# three wires, and a stage on a wire that is not the deepest
def poly with Circuits = dup ; _ dup ; _ _ add1 ; _ * ; +
#   poly : Int =Circuits Recursive> Int — x + x·(x+1)
def polyOut in Circuits = poly ; observe
polyOut ; print             # 39 — (3 + 3·4) + (4 + 4·5)

# ---- a carrier built BY HAND: `in Circuits` -------------------------
# `sumFrom` is the whole reason the Arrow exists — a stateful circuit
# that is not `embed` of anything.  Before 5c½ a def like `0 ; sumFrom`
# was STRANDED: it produces a `Circuit(Int, Int)` at the base, but the
# elaborator would not treat it as a word of the category, because doing
# so would mean reading an INFERRED type to decide what a word is.
#
# `in Circuits` is the written declaration that closes it.  It applies
# nothing — no embedding, no composition, no transport — it says "this
# def is a morphism of the category Circuits presents", and the
# elaborator CHECKS the claim against the def's arrow: a word of a
# category builds one carrier out of nothing.  The claim is written, the
# check is mechanical, and the def joins the K-word table beside the
# ones `with Circuits` transported.
def sum0 in Circuits = 0 ; sumFrom
#   sum0 : • =Recursive> Circuit(Int, Int) — UNFOLDED, and no `Circuits` on it.
#
# That is the honest reading and it is worth the sentence.  A receipt is
# PROVENANCE: `=Circuits>` says the code went through the functor.  `sum0`
# did not — it is a circuit no base program denotes, which is the whole
# reason Arrows exist — so claiming the label would claim membership in
# the functor's IMAGE, which is a strictly stronger thing.  `in` mints
# nothing, because `in` applies nothing; only a `with` mints, and every
# `with` does.  Membership is carried by the carrier in the type and the
# entry in the K-word table.
#
# Under the marker it composes like any other word of the category, and
# THAT composite went through the functor, so it folds:
def running with Circuits = sum0 ; dbl
#   running : Int =Circuits Recursive> Int
def runningOut in Circuits = running ; observe
runningOut ; print          # 20 — 3 ⇒ 3 ⇒ 6, then 4 ⇒ 7 ⇒ 14

# ---- over/use coherence, as a law -------------------------------------
# `with` writes out what `in` writes by hand, so the two must denote
# the same morphism.  Here is the statement at a stateful pair, where
# the claim has teeth: `sum0` and `scale3` are both built by hand.
def scale3 in Circuits = 3 ; scale

def byHand in Circuits = sum0 scale3 ; compose
def byUse with Circuits = sum0 ; scale3
#   byHand : • =Recursive> Circuit(Int, Int)   — no label: nothing was applied
#   byUse  : Int =Circuits Recursive> Int      — transported, so it folds

def handObs in Circuits = byHand ; observe
def useObs in Circuits = byUse  ; observe
handObs ; print             # 30 — 3 ⇒ 3 ⇒ 9, then 4 ⇒ 7 ⇒ 21
useObs  ; print             # 30 — the same circuit, said two ways

# `sameCode` cannot DECIDE it, and the reason is the same one the laws
# hit below: composing circuits applies a program that arrived as a
# wire, and `ev` of a value that is not a literal quotation is outside
# the structural fragment.  So coherence is observed, not proved — the
# honest state of the art for an Arrow, and exactly why `observe` is a
# slot of the theory.

# ---- the corollary: a SEALED category ---------------------------------
# Exits are the only way out, so a theory that declares none is sealed:
# every stage inside the scope is embedded and the only word that
# touches the accumulated carrier is the composition, which hands back a
# carrier.  Nothing written in the category can leave it, and that is
# abstract-type sealing falling out of the same rule.
data Capsule(a, b) = Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩

theory Vault(k(_, _)) in Doctrine =
    embed   : Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩ ⇒ k(a, b)
    compose : k(a, b) k(b, c) ⇒ k(a, c)

def capThen = (f g -> [(x -> f ; unCapsule ; _ x ; ev ; (y -> g ; unCapsule ; _ y ; ev))] ; Capsule)

model Sealed in Vault(Capsule) =
    embed   = Capsule
    compose = capThen

def guarded with Sealed = add1 ; dbl
def guarded2 with Sealed = guarded ; add1
#   guarded2 : Int =Sealed> Int — and the only consumer of a Sealed
#   word, inside the category's vocabulary, is another Sealed word.
#
# `Vault` takes the doctrine's `compose` and `embed` and NOT its
# `first`, so every stage of a `with Sealed` scope must be one wire in
# and one wire out: `with Sealed ; dup ; *` is refused, naming the slot
# that would have carried it.  That is the level table, read straight
# off what the theory declared.
#
# It declares no `observe` and no `sample` either, so NO doctrine law
# runs for `Sealed`: an inherited law runs when the model can state it,
# and a sealed category admits no observation.  That is the honest
# reading of sealed, and it is worth seeing beside `Circuits`, where all
# seven run.

# The honest caveat: the CARRIER's own unroller is an ordinary base word
# and Braid has no export lists, so `unCapsule` still opens it from
# outside.  A model seals the CATEGORY — nothing written in it leaves it
# by any route the model offers — and sealing the TYPE is a separate,
# unshipped question.
guarded2 ; unCapsule ; _ 5 ; ev ; print    # 13 — ((5+1)·2)+1

# ---- what the laws can and cannot decide ------------------------------
# The seven category axioms — leftId, rightId, assoc, embedFunctor,
# firstFst, firstEmbed, firstCompose — are the PRELUDE's, inherited by
# `in Doctrine` and run for `Circuits` and for `Funcs`.  This file
# used to write five of them out; the doctrine writes them once.
#
# They run through `observe` at sample points — and since 2026-09-13
# the two models answer differently when asked to PROVE them instead.
#
# `Funcs` proves six of the seven.  Its composition applies a program
# that arrived as a wire, and `Arr(a, b)` unrolls to `Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩` — one
# wire in, one wire out — so the normalizer can give that `ev` an
# arity, and `[lhs] [rhs] ; sameCode` answers `true` for leftId,
# rightId, assoc, embedFunctor, firstEmbed and firstCompose.  Only
# `firstFst` is left: it is stated at THREE pairings, and the pairing's
# instantiation is lost when a generic quotation body (`compose`'s) is
# weighed on its own, so the wire the law would `unPair` comes back as
# a bare variable.
#
# `Circuits` proves none of them, and the reason is structural rather
# than incidental: `compC` applies the self wire of a `fix`, whose
# arrow is `Fn⟨Σ =Recursive> Θ⟩` with Σ an open stack variable — there is no
# arity to give THAT.  Equality modulo a model's defining equations is
# structural induction over an initial algebra, a different procedure
# from normalizing in a free category (design-macros.md, "honest
# gaps").  So for codata the laws stay finite tests of an infinite
# object, which is what `observe` was asked for in the first place —
# and `Sealed` below, whose composition is `unCapsule ; ev`, proves its
# axioms the way `Funcs` does.

# The laws ran before any of this printed — seven of them, twice, for
# two models with nothing in common but the doctrine.  `firstFst` is
# the one worth
# a second look: `first f ; arr fst = arr fst ; f` is a naturality
# square, and stating it needed `k` at THREE different pairs of wires —
# `k(Int, Pair(Int, Int))`, `k(Pair(Int, Int), Pair(Int, Int))`,
# `k(Pair(Int, Int), Int)`. A wire parameter could not have said it,
# which is why `theory Pipeline(a)` (what this file used to hold) was a
# category with ONE object and this one is not.
